<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Wajo Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 CE – 1906 CE (approx.) · capital Tosora</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Bugis kingdom of South Sulawesi with an elected ruler and a famous tradition of freedom, whose merchants traded across the archipelago.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Wajo grew in the 15th century around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tosora">Tosora</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tempe">Lake Tempe</a>, from a union of farming communities. Its chronicles describe a founding agreement between ruler and people that guaranteed the people's freedoms, summed up in the saying that the people of Wajo are free and only custom is their master.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Wajo's head, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arung-matoa">Arung Matoa</a>, was elected by a council of lords and could be removed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tadampare-puang-ri-magalatung">La Tadampare Puang ri Ma'galatung</a>, in the 16th century, is remembered as its wisest ruler. In 1582 Wajo joined Bone and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/soppeng-kingdom">Soppeng</a> in the Tellumpoccoe, but in 1610 Gowa conquered it and imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Wajo merchants built networks from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Wajo backed Gowa in the war of 1666 to 1669 and suffered when Gowa lost. In the 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-maddukelleng">La Maddukelleng</a> returned from exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> to fight Bone and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. The Dutch conquered Wajo in 1906, and it is now a regency of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1450 CE – 1906 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.56%;width:9.07%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 CE – 1906 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tosora</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>La Tadampare Puang ri Ma'galatung, La Maddukelleng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bugis</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wajo, Wajoq</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=wajo-kingdom" aria-label="Wajo Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8013 2332 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wajo Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8333 2588L8312 2628M8333 2588L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8312 2628h0M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8333 2588h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8333 2588h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tosora. <a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=wajo-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

